"""Research-grounded interpretation guide accompanying the visual report."""
from pathlib import Path


def write_quality_guide(root, run=None):
    rows = [
        ("Global semantic features", "Frozen linear probe; kNN", "Accuracy, macro-F1, balanced accuracy; per-class confusion", "Primary evidence for classification. Fit/select on train/validation and report held-out test scores."),
        ("Dense spatial features", "Frozen patch-feature segmentation probe", "mIoU and per-class IoU; boundary quality when relevant", "Primary evidence for spatial representation quality. Compare the same head, resolution and training budget."),
        ("Retrieval and local neighborhoods", "Nearest-neighbor image galleries; object/instance retrieval", "Recall@K or mAP when positive matches are defined", "Shows what the model regards as similar. Exclude the query and control duplicates/source groups."),
        ("Spatial feature structure", "Shared-basis patch PCA; patch-to-patch correspondence maps", "Qualitative views; correspondence accuracy when annotated", "Useful for boundaries and matching parts. PCA RGB colors are feature directions, not semantic predictions."),
        ("Internal attention", "Last-layer attention; individual heads; attention rollout", "Qualitative maps; optional pointing/foreground-overlap tests with suitable annotations", "Attention is token mixing. Generic CLS attention is not class-conditioned and does not prove causal importance."),
        ("Clustering structure", "K-means or another explicitly chosen clustering method", "ARI/NMI/AMI against labels; silhouette for separation; seed/K stability", "Supplementary evidence. These are not accuracy percentages; classes need not form one compact cluster each."),
        ("Feature health / collapse checks", "Feature variance; covariance eigenspectrum; pairwise similarity", "Effective rank / participation ratio; variance and similarity distributions", "Detects degeneracy or reduced dimensional diversity. There is no universal threshold establishing good downstream quality."),
        ("Original versus adapted backbone", "Compare paired features on the same images", "CKA; neighborhood overlap; downstream score differences", "Measures similarity/change between representations. Higher or lower CKA alone does not mean better features."),
        ("2D / 3D exploration", "PCA, UMAP and t-SNE", "Exploratory visualization, not a model-ranking score", "Separate fits have different axes. Projection gaps and attractive blobs do not establish held-out generalization."),
    ]
    table = "".join("<tr>" + "".join(f"<td>{value}</td>" for value in row) + "</tr>" for row in rows)
    page = '''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>How to evaluate backbone representations</title><style>body{font:16px system-ui;line-height:1.6;color:#18283c;background:#f4f7fa;max-width:1350px;margin:30px auto;padding:0 22px}table{border-collapse:collapse;background:white;width:100%;font-size:14px}td,th{padding:14px;border:1px solid #dbe3eb;text-align:left;vertical-align:top}th{background:#102b42;color:white}a{color:#0874a7}.scroll{overflow:auto}li{margin:10px 0}</style></head><body><a href="index.html">Back to all models</a><h1>How researchers evaluate backbone representations</h1><p>A useful evaluation combines task performance, spatial inspection and feature diagnostics. There is no single “visual quality” number. DINO combines linear/kNN evaluation with attention inspection; DINOv2 also evaluates image-level and dense prediction tasks and illustrates patch PCA.</p><div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Check</th><th>Typical metrics</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody>TABLE</tbody></table></div><h2>Recommended priority for SeaDino</h2><ol><li>Use the existing held-out linear/kNN macro-F1 and segmentation mIoU tables as the main quantitative comparison.</li><li>Inspect identical images with patch PCA, last-layer attention, individual heads and rollout where defined. Compare full-frame and organism-crop conditions explicitly.</li><li>Use retrieval galleries, feature variance/rank and clustering stability to diagnose why downstream behavior changes.</li></ol><h2>Reading the attention maps</h2><p>DINOv3, SeaDino, DINOv2 and CLIP have a learned CLS token: their maps show final-layer CLS-to-patch attention, averaged over heads, alongside individual heads. Rollout adds identity residuals, normalizes rows and multiplies head-averaged attention matrices across layers. Register tokens participate in rollout but are excluded from spatial output maps.</p><p>SAM3.1 and MarineInst do not have a learned CLS token. Their views show attention received by spatial keys, averaged uniformly over spatial queries in the last global encoder layer. These maps have different semantics from CLS attention; a synthetic mean-pooled adapter token is never treated as a native CLS query. CLS rollout is not fabricated for windowed patch-only encoders.</p><p>Overlays use per-image percentile contrast for visibility. A bright pixel does not imply the same attention probability across images or models. Raw unscaled arrays and metadata are saved. Dashed cyan boxes identify dataset annotations, not predictions.</p><h2>Current FathomNet caveat</h2><p>The current report uses whole frames labeled by one annotated organism. The selected box covers a median 1.2% of the frame in this sample. Weak agreement between four K-means groups and four organism labels can coexist with useful neighborhood/probe performance. This visualization set contains training samples; these diagnostics are not final test scores.</p><h2>Primary sources</h2><ul><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.14294">DINO: Emerging Properties in Self-Supervised Vision Transformers</a> — kNN/linear evaluation and emergent attention structure.</li><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.07193">DINOv2: Learning Robust Visual Features without Supervision</a> — global/dense evaluations and patch-feature PCA.</li><li><a href="https://aclanthology.org/2020.acl-main.385/">Abnar &amp; Zuidema: Quantifying Attention Flow in Transformers</a> — attention rollout.</li><li><a href="https://proceedings.mlr.press/v97/kornblith19a.html">Kornblith et al.: Similarity of Neural Network Representations Revisited</a> — CKA.</li><li><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.04906">VICReg: Variance-Invariance-Covariance Regularization</a> — variance/covariance and collapse prevention.</li><li><a href="https://umap-learn.readthedocs.io/en/latest/clustering.html">UMAP documentation: Using UMAP for Clustering</a> — projection and density caveats.</li></ul></body></html>'''.replace("TABLE", table)
    if run and run.get("source_name") == "coralscapes":
        start = page.index('<h2>Current FathomNet caveat</h2>')
        end = page.index('<h2>Primary sources</h2>', start)
        page = page[:start] + '<h2>Current Coralscapes view</h2><p>The selected training images have dense semantic annotations. Original masks and the official class palette are displayed beside feature PCA and attention; masks never enter extraction or PCA fitting. White PCA boundaries are annotation outlines. These views support qualitative spatial inspection, while held-out segmentation mIoU requires fitting a separate semantic head. Generic attention is not expected to reproduce a multiclass mask.</p><p><a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/EPFL-ECEO/coralscapes">Coralscapes dataset and official class palette</a></p>' + page[end:]
    (Path(root) / "quality_guide.html").write_text(page)
